3.	PowerGlove‘ Keypad Control

The PowerGlove‘ contains a keypad on the cuff of the glove.  The keys on this keypad can be used for a variety of uses, ranging from user input of data to control "hot keys" to enable certain events.  Given this immense flexibility of use, the PGSI also uses the keypad to control internal settings while in a special mode called Setup mode.  A user in setup mode can change attributes like filter settings, emulation mode, and continuous/request settings.  Since this mode changes vital information in the PGSI, it can only be initiated from the keypad using a sequence of keys.  This prevents the user from accidentally enabling Setup mode.

To change from Normal mode to Setup mode, the user must hold down the PROG key and release it, immediately press down the 'A' key and hold until the glove beeps to signal it is again receiving data.  Since the program key resets the PowerGlove‘, it is necessary to position the PowerGlove‘ in front of the receivers to allow the glove to synchronize with the receivers, and the user will have to re-center the glove as well as retrain the finger flex system.  Note that upon pressing the 'A' key, the user will hear a faint beep.  This beep is not the one signaling new data.

The PowerGlove‘ Serial Interface defaults to what is known as AGE emulation normal mode.  This is one of 4 modes the interface can be in at any time.  This mode allows all commands associated with the AGE PowerGlove‘ Serial Adapter to be used without modification.  In this mode the keypad keys can be read under software control via the packets of information returned from the PGSI.  The modes are described below:

AGE Normal mode:	Information packet and command set conforms to AGE Specification. Keypad keys correspond to values in KEYS byte in data packet.

AGE Setup mode:	Information packet and command set conforms to AGE Specification. Keypad keys correspond to values listed in Table 2.1.1

Menelli Normal mode:	Named after the originator of the freeware code for the HC11/PowerGlove‘ setup using these commands.  Information packet and command set conforms to Ron Menelli's specification in his code.  Keypad keys correspond to values in KEYS byte in data packet.

Menelli Setup mode:	Information packet and command set correspond to Ron Menelli's specification in his code.  Keypad keys correspond to values listed in Table 3.3.1.


